---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { components } from "../data/standards";
---

<BaseLayout>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <img class="hero-image" src="/assets/governance-hero.png" alt="Modular governance controls and repositories arranged as connected infrastructure" />
    <div class="hero-scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="hero-content">
      <p class="eyebrow">Governancecommons.org</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Governance Commons</h1>
      <p class="hero-copy">A maintainer-led sociotechnical commons for inspectable governance of human–AI and multi-agent systems.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="#components">Explore components</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="#mission">Why governance needs a commons</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <aside class="hero-panel" aria-label="Governance Commons public status">
      <div><span class="metric">3</span><span class="metric-label">released specifications</span></div>
      <div><span class="metric">v1.2.0</span><span class="metric-label">pinned baseline</span></div>
      <div><span class="metric">No</span><span class="metric-label">certification authority</span></div>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Current public state</p>
      <h2>RFC-0001 is accepted; its new public boundary is not yet active.</h2>
      <p>The accepted Public Boundary Model separates the umbrella, independently adoptable specifications, reference tooling, publication infrastructure, and maintainer workspace. Activation still depends on gates G-1 through G-5. GC v1.2.0 remains the current pinned release record while that transition is verified.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions" style="margin-top:24px">
      <a class="button ink" href="/release">Read release and transition status</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/governancecommons/governance-commons/blob/main/rfcs/RFC-0001-public-boundary-model.md">Read RFC-0001</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="mission" class="section white">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">The sociotechnical problem</p>
      <h2>Capability is not the same as legitimate authority.</h2>
      <p>Governed systems need more than schemas or agent routing. They need inspectable answers about identity, authority, delegation, human oversight, handoffs, provenance, evidence, accountability, institutions, trust, legitimacy, power, appeal, override, revocation, and human–agent coordination.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="intro-grid">
      <article class="surface"><h3>Authority before action</h3><p>Record who may act, under what scope, from which source of authority, and how that authority expires or is revoked.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><h3>Evidence after action</h3><p>Preserve validation, handoff, provenance, telemetry, and decision evidence without pretending that evidence itself grants authority.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><h3>Human recourse</h3><p>Keep approval, refusal, appeal, override, escalation, and accountability visible when work crosses human and institutional boundaries.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="boundary" class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Accepted boundary model</p>
      <h2>Five public roles, not five equal standards.</h2>
      <p>This is the transition authorized by RFC-0001. It becomes active doctrine only after all activation gates verify.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="standards-grid">
      <article class="surface"><span class="step">01</span><h3>Public umbrella</h3><p>Mission, shared vocabulary, publication identity, and community-facing orientation. Citeable, not adoptable.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><span class="step">02</span><h3>Specifications</h3><p>Agent Dossier, Agent Matrix, and ONS are independently versioned adoption units.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><span class="step">03</span><h3>Reference tooling</h3><p>Validators, SDKs, CLIs, evidence utilities, and runtime candidates implement selected contracts.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><span class="step">04</span><h3>Publication infrastructure</h3><p>Websites and repositories publish source, releases, documentation, schemas, and status.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><span class="step">05</span><h3>Maintainer workspace</h3><p>Plans, release records, internal coordination, and construction history are not products.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="components" class="section white">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Public components</p>
      <h2>Role, maturity, version, and baseline status stay visible.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="standards-grid">
      {components.map((component) => (
        <article class="surface standard-card">
          <div class="standard-topline"><span>{component.category}</span><span>{component.maturity}</span></div>
          <h3>{component.name}</h3>
          <p><strong>{component.role}</strong></p>
          <p>{component.scope}</p>
          <p class="standard-detail">v{component.version} · {component.baseline}</p>
          <div class="standard-footer"><code>{component.shortName}</code><a class="text-link" href={`/standards/${component.slug}`}>View status →</a></div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="adopt" class="section tint">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Adopt</p>
      <h2>Make a narrow claim that another person can inspect.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="adopt-grid">
      <article class="surface"><span class="step">01</span><h3>Name</h3><p>Choose one released specification and pin the exact version you implement.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><span class="step">02</span><h3>Verify</h3><p>Use a published validator only where it supports that target. Otherwise document manual checks and label the result provisional.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><span class="step">03</span><h3>Preserve</h3><p>Retain the validator version, ConformanceReport or manual evidence, source provenance, and limits of the claim.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="actions" style="justify-content:center; margin-top:30px"><a class="button ink" href="/conformance">Validation and declaration guidance</a></div>
  </section>

  <section id="commons" class="section white">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research, education, and community</p>
      <h2>The public purpose is broader than the repositories.</h2>
      <p>Today, GC publishes specifications, evidence contracts, reference tooling, an ecosystem map, and maintainer-led explanatory material. The goals below are directions for a commons—not claims that formal programs or neutral institutions already exist.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="intro-grid">
      <article class="surface"><h3>Research</h3><p>Connect technical mechanisms to sociology, institutions, legitimacy, power, trust, accountability, and the real effects of delegated machine action.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><h3>Education</h3><p>Develop approachable examples, curricula, case studies, and critical vocabulary for builders, reviewers, organizations, and affected communities.</p></article>
      <article class="surface"><h3>Community</h3><p>Invite critique, implementation experience, competing perspectives, and future shared stewardship without claiming neutrality or participation that does not yet exist.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section contact-section">
    <div class="contact-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Authority boundary</p><h2>A commons, not a regulator or certifier.</h2><p>Governance Commons is not ISO, NIST, IEEE, AAIF, a regulator, or a certification authority. External organizations retain authority over their own work; GC must preserve their provenance and state relationships as alignment, mapping, implementation, or research—not endorsement.</p></div>
    <div class="contact-actions"><a class="button primary" href="/ecosystem">Explore the ecosystem boundary</a></div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
